 ## Subtopics
 
-### Bilateral Filter
-A non-linear filter used to reduce noise while preserving edges, by weighting pixels based on both spatial proximity and value similarity [S2, unofficial]. Unlike the Gaussian filter which only uses spatial distance, the Bilateral filter also uses value similarity, preserving edges better [S2, unofficial]. It is defined as [S2, unofficial]:
-$$ BF(p) = \frac{1}{W_p} \sum_{q \in S} G_{\sigma_s}(\|p - q\|) G_{\sigma_r}(\|I_p - I_q\|) I_q $$
-where $W_p$ is the normalization factor [S2, unofficial].
-- $\sigma_s$: Spatial standard deviation, controls the decrease in influence based on distance.
-- $\sigma_r$: Range standard deviation, controls the decrease in influence based on value difference.
-- Typical values: $\sigma_s = 4$, $\sigma_r = 0.2$ [S2, unofficial].
+### [Bilateral Filter](8983-bilateral-filter.md)
+A non-linear filter used to reduce noise while preserving edges by weighting pixels based on both spatial proximity and value similarity. Unlike the [Separable 3D Gaussian Filter](8956-separable-3d-gaussian-filter.md) which only uses spatial distance, the Bilateral filter also uses value similarity, preserving edges better.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The separable approach reduces the complexity from $O(N \cdot K^3)$ for a direct 3D convolution to $O(N \cdot 3K)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels and $K$ is the kernel size [S1, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Interactive rates are achieved using a CUDA implementation [S1, unofficial]. Table 1 in [S1, unofficial] provides specific timings for a CT body (256 × 256 × 415), CT head (512 × 512 × 297), and salmon (336 × 173 × 511) with different radii. For example, the total time for the CT body with a specific radius is 617.24 ms [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Interactive performance due to separability; effective noise reduction [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Blurs edges compared to non-linear filters like the Bilateral filter [S2, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- Time Complexity: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, due to the separability of the Gaussian kernel. The cost is proportional to the number of voxels and the kernel radius [S1, unofficial].
+- Memory Complexity: $O(N)$ for storing the volume and intermediate results [S1, unofficial].
 
+**Measured Performance:**
+- Performance data is provided for CT body (256 × 256 × 415), CT head (512 × 512 × 297), and salmon (336 × 173 × 511) datasets with different kernel radii [S1, unofficial].
+- The total time includes 3 convolutions, subtraction, and additional operations like memory allocations and transfers [S1, unofficial].
+- Specific times are reported in milliseconds in Table 1 of the source [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- Strengths: Interactive performance due to separability and CUDA implementation [S1, unofficial].
+- Limitations: Does not preserve edges as well as non-linear filters like the Bilateral filter [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when computational efficiency is critical and edge preservation is not the primary concern [S1, unofficial].
+- Use for computing spatial importance maps in volume illustration [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+- Do not use when edge preservation is crucial [S1, unofficial].
 ## Background
-The method is part of a pipeline for interactive volume illustration, where it is used to compute a spatial importance map to modulate color and opacity assigned by transfer functions [S1, unofficial]. It was presented in the context of Computer Graphics, specifically Transfer Functions and Volume Rendering [S1, unofficial].
-
+The method is described in the context of interactive volume illustration, where it is used to compute spatial importance maps to modulate color and opacity assigned by transfer functions [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages the separability of the Gaussian kernel to achieve interactive rates using a CUDA implementation [S1, unofficial].
